\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N$, $\mathbb Z$, $\mathbb Q$, $\mathbb R$, and $\mathbb C$ denote the natural numbers, integers, rationals, reals, and complex numbers, respectively. All additional parameters and hypotheses are specified in the question.


\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Suppose a metric space $X$ is doubling, meaning that every ball can be covered by a fixed finite number of balls of half its radius. If $X$ admits a bi-Lipschitz embedding into a Hilbert space, must it admit a bi-Lipschitz embedding into $\mathbb R^m$ for some finite $m$?
\par\smallskip\textit{Formulation references:} \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}, \href{http://aimpl.org/mappingmetric/2/}{[S2]}.

\subsection{Short English statement}
Suppose a metric space $X$ is doubling, meaning that every ball can be covered by a fixed finite number of balls of half its radius. If $X$ admits a bi-Lipschitz embedding into a Hilbert space, must it admit a bi-Lipschitz embedding into $\mathbb R^m$ for some finite $m$?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems, record 20000582 (AIM-ANALYSIS-0114). Catalogue attribution under CC BY 4.0.
